---
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import ArticleFaq from '../../../components/ArticleFaq.astro';
import CTASection from '../../../components/CTASection.astro';
import { MANUFACTURER, CERTIFICATIONS, RELATIONSHIP } from '../../../data/site';

const title = 'How to Audit a Lead-Acid Battery Factory | Dingwei Battery';
const description = 'How to audit a lead-acid battery factory for OEM sourcing: certifications, production capability, quality control, testing and export readiness.';
const articleTitle = 'How to audit a lead-acid battery factory';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Do I need to visit the factory in person?', a: 'Not always. A documented audit — certificates, process descriptions and test data — plus a production sample covers most of the risk. But for a large program, an on-site audit is worth the trip.' },
  { q: 'What is the most telling thing to look at?', a: 'Curing time. A factory that rushes curing to save days produces weak plates that fail early — ask for their curing window, not just their capacity numbers.' },
  { q: 'Why is a lead-acid audit simpler than a lithium audit?', a: 'Lead-acid needs no thermal-runaway or cell-safety audit. The audit focus shifts to process consistency and raw-material control, which are easier to verify.' },
];
---
<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} articleTitle={articleTitle} articleSection="Quality" dateModified="2026-09-03" faqs={faqs}>
  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge</span>
    <h1>How to audit a lead-acid battery factory</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-01 · Source: Chengguang Energy technical documentation</p>
    <p class="lead">A factory audit is how you verify that a battery manufacturer can deliver your specification repeatably — not just once. Run these checks before committing volume.</p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Audit a battery factory against five verifiable areas: current certifications, production capacity versus your volume, in-process and end-of-line quality control, batch traceability, and export documentation. A credible manufacturer shows current certificates, a recent production sample, and a specification sheet rather than sales claims alone.
    </div>

    <h2>Certifications</h2>
    <p>Request current certificates, not claims. {MANUFACTURER.name} holds {CERTIFICATIONS.join(', ')}.</p>

    <h2>Production capability</h2>
    <p>Check factory area, number of production lines and daily output against your annual volume. {MANUFACTURER.name} operates a {MANUFACTURER.factoryArea} plant with {MANUFACTURER.productionLines} producing {MANUFACTURER.dailyOutput}.</p>

    <h2>Quality control &amp; testing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Incoming material inspection</li>
      <li>In-process checks (grid casting, pasting, curing, formation)</li>
      <li>100% end-of-line electrical test</li>
      <li>Batch traceability</li>
      <li>Lab testing for CCA and capacity</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Export readiness</h2>
    <p>Confirm export experience, documentation capability and destination markets. {MANUFACTURER.name} exports to {MANUFACTURER.exportMarkets}.</p>

    <h2>Audit scorecard</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Certification</strong> — current certificates are visible and verifiable.</li>
      <li><strong>Capacity</strong> — plant area, lines and output map to your annual volume.</li>
      <li><strong>Quality</strong> — in-process and end-of-line tests are documented.</li>
      <li><strong>Export</strong> — destination markets and document capability are clear.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Use the same four columns for every factory, then compare against the <a href="/factory/">Dingwei factory overview</a> as a worked example.</p>

    <h2>What to bring home from an audit</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Current certificates</li>
      <li>Sample of a recent production run</li>
      <li>A specification sheet for your reference model</li>
      <li>Agreed commercial terms</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-supplier-checklist/">OEM battery supplier checklist &rarr;</a> · <a href="/factory/">Battery factory capability &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>{RELATIONSHIP}</p>

    <h2>The ten stages to audit</h2>
    <p>Walk the factory in the order the battery is made. For each stage, ask what is measured and how rejects are controlled.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Grid casting</strong> — check grid thickness and alloy consistency.</li>
      <li><strong>Paste mixing</strong> — check mix control and batch records.</li>
      <li><strong>Pasting</strong> — check plate weight and paste application.</li>
      <li><strong>Curing</strong> — check time and conditions; a typical window is 2–4 days.</li>
      <li><strong>Assembly</strong> — check plate alignment and separator handling.</li>
      <li><strong>Welding</strong> — check inter-cell and terminal welds.</li>
      <li><strong>Electrolyte filling</strong> — check acid volume and density control.</li>
      <li><strong>Formation charging</strong> — check the charging profile and duration.</li>
      <li><strong>Sealing</strong> — check lid, vent and leak control.</li>
      <li><strong>Testing</strong> — confirm 100% end-of-line electrical test plus CCA and capacity sampling.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Take this sequence with you, and compare it to the documented checks on the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-supplier-checklist/">OEM battery supplier checklist</a>.</p>

    <h2>Lead-acid audit is simpler than lithium</h2>
    <p>Lead-acid audit effort concentrates on process consistency and raw materials. A lithium-cell audit adds cell safety and thermal-runaway controls; lead-acid does not carry the same cell-safety audit burden. That makes the lead-acid audit shorter and easier to standardize for OEM buyers.</p>
    <p>{RELATIONSHIP} Review the factory footprint at <a href="/factory/">Dingwei factory overview</a> as a worked example of the audit data to request.</p>
  </section>

  <ArticleFaq faqs={faqs} />

  <CTASection title="Put Dingwei Battery through your audit" />
</ArticleLayout>
